A||||11||stimpy||Why does my car smell hot?||||1||
Z||000000||stimpy||10-23-2001||11:34 PM||||Lately when I've been driving my car, usually within about 5 miles from starting out, the inside of my car starts to smell like coolant. Altough my temp guage is still inoperable, I doubt it's actually overheating. When I open both front and rear compartments, I don't smell it then, and no water is boiling up in the overflow.<P>Does this mean my heater core is taking a dump on me?<P>Help me Mr. Wizard!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Pontiaddict||10-23-2001||11:51 PM||||The heater core would be a good place to check.<P>And I only looked in this topic so I can make a stupid response like...<BR>"It smells so hot becasue of that chanel no5 that you sprayed in the interior." <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cheever3000||10-24-2001||12:04 AM||||That was the first symptom I had when the heater core went out on my Coupe.  The next one was seeing a mist in the air, like condensation.<P>If you swap my alternator & belt, I'll swap out your heater core.  But -- how would we meet?!  <P>Get under the dash on the passenger side and drop the bottom of the box.  First, put some old towels on the floor, cause my bet is you'll have coolant coming out before you get to the last screws.<P>Heater cores don't cost much.  The only one in stock in my town was at O'Reilly's.  But it's still in there and working fine.  Don't listen to those jerks who say it's the easiest repair there is on a Fiero -- there's some real tight places on both sides of the dash for guys our size to reach into.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Bugsy||10-24-2001||04:45 AM||||Well, isn't this a coincidence, i've been smelling antifreeze for a couple of weeks, and knew what was coming next....i have my own mobile steam room <P>------------------<BR>86 SE I'm addicted and proud of it<BR>Kevin||||reg||9
Z||000004||California Kid||10-24-2001||06:25 AM||||You probably have a small pinhole leak in the heatercore, as you didn't mention any severe loss of coolant.  If you want to buy some time before replacing it, pick up some "stop leak" for the radiator (it's almost powder like in a small container, that you add to coolant in radiator), works very well for small leaks. If you have an oily film on the inside of the windows, it may be a little bigger than a small hole.<P>Because of the work involved in changing the heatercore, it's wise to have the replacement part pressure checked before you put it in.||||reg||1
Z||000005||DJRice||10-24-2001||07:24 AM||||I think both Archie and WCF sell performance cores, but I'm not sure which is better.  But that's a different thread, isn't it?<P>--Dillon||||reg||1
Z||000006||stimpy||10-24-2001||09:51 AM||||Well, doesn't Archie's heater core put out more BTU's then the ventilation controls can adequately handle? Or should I go with West Coast's newer style and replace the ventilation from a late model donor?||||reg||1
Z||000007||roadwarrior||10-24-2001||09:58 AM||||just make sure to change it before you start getting a mist, that stuff is poisenous<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||stimpy||10-24-2001||02:43 PM||||Thanks for the concern, roadwarrior. Just got back from the shop where I got a new heater core put in. Poisonous is one of those words that make me pay attention...<P>Oh, and for any other Oklahoma City Fiero owners, I want to plug the shop who does the work I'm to lazy to do and bargain prices, Precision Tune on South Western. George, the owner, is a great guy and actually really likes Fieros even if he does drive Fords.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Formula88||10-24-2001||04:35 PM||||Well, I'm a big dude, and had no trouble at all changing the heater core in my 88.  I kept thinking "it can't be THIS easy" as I was doing it.  The hardest part was getting the hoses on and off the heater core.  The core R&R inside the car was a snap.  Took about 20 minutes, and I'd never done one before.||||reg||6
Z||000010||Formula88||10-24-2001||04:39 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DJRice:<BR><B>I think both Archie and WCF sell performance cores, but I'm not sure which is better.  But that's a different thread, isn't it?<P>--Dillon</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, Archie's heater core requires you to cut a hole in the side of the dashboard for the hose fitting to stick out.  But boy does it HEAT!!!  <P>The WCF core, is the same size as the original unit, but is polished aluminum and held in place by $100 custom made poly mounts and has a turbine mounted to it to force feed the coolant.  They claim it's faster, um, I mean heats better than a Small Block heater.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000011||Cheever3000||10-24-2001||06:58 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR>The core R&R inside the car was a snap.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE>What about that one screw way up there that you can't even see?  I was darn lucky to reach it and get it out.  Had to ask my skinny, lanky son to put it back in.<P>I'm glad it worked for you, though.  I'll do another one if I have to, but wouldn't look forward to it.<P><BR>||||reg||1
